  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSteady rain with embedded heavier downpours and perhaps a few \nrumbles of thunder should taper off and come to an end this\nmorning from west to east, with drying out starting across\nwestern zones around 12z. Breaks of sun are likely across New \nHampshire during the day bringing the chance for scattered pop \nup showers during the afternoon hours. Overall rainfall amounts \ngenerally look to fall in the 0.5 to 1.00 in range although a\nfew areas with more sustained downpours may receive upwards of\n1.5 inches. Otherwise, low clouds and some drizzle may very well\npersist coastal zones much of the day, especially from Portland\non north and northeast. Warmest temperatures today where breaks\nof sun are expected in southwestern NH. May have to lower the \ntemperature forecast further outside of our southwestern NH \nzones due to cool northeast flow. \n\nWith light winds and a wet ground likely for tonight, fog is\nlikely, with areas of it possibly being dense.\n\nA better day is foreseen Friday after early morning stratus and\nfog burns off and temperatures rising well into the 70s to lower\n80s. However, with continued troughing overhead, scattered \nshowers remain possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper low will slowly slide southward from central Quebec through \nnorthern Maine and over to Nova Scotia Friday into Saturday. A \nsurface low looks to stall over the Canadian Maritimes, allowing for \nthe development of some retrograding showers Friday evening. Showers \nmay even continue through the day on Saturday as weak instability \nremains and moisture continues to get advected from the northeast. A \nfew rumbles of thunder can't be ruled out Saturday afternoon. By \nSunday morning, the surface low over Nova Scotia is expected to \nshift to the southeast. This will allow for high pressure to move in \nfrom the northwest and a period of tranquil weather is likely Sunday \nthrough most of Monday. Persistent northwest flow will lead to \ncooler temperatures and dewpoints through the holiday weekend with \nhighs mainly in the upper 60s north to the mid 70s south and some \nbreezier winds out of the north. Unsettled weather may return \ntowards the middle of next week.",
